# bead bpp (amendment, coordinator-requested 2026-09-15): give w3a-rsz-2 a bounded post-CTS /
# post-GRT resizer-timing repair, so a stuck design (one unfixable violator that repair keeps
# retrying without progress) cannot loop near-indefinitely and block the flow. Motivated by
# w3a's attempt 2, which reached repair_timing iteration 1040 in 73 min with 27550 violators
# unchanged, pinned on one sram_controller flop.
#
# Two opt-in hooks, both byte-identical to upstream when unset:
#
#   PL_RESIZER_TIMING_MAX_PASSES (int, optional) -- appended as `-max_passes N` to BOTH the
#   setup and hold repair_timing calls in rsz_timing_postcts.tcl and rsz_timing_postgrt.tcl.
#   Confirmed present with identical semantics (bounds the coarse outer sweep count passed to
#   rsz::repair_setup / rsz::repair_hold; default 10000 either way) on both the bundled OpenROAD
#   (edf00dff) and OpenROAD 26Q2.
#
#   PL_RESIZER_TIMING_EXTRA_ARGS (str, optional) -- a Tcl word list spliced verbatim onto the
#   end of the SETUP repair_timing call ONLY (not hold), via `{*}$::env(...)`. Added because
#   -max_passes alone does not bound WALL-CLOCK time for an oscillating design: a "pass" is a
#   coarse unit, and a single pass over a design that never converges can itself run for a very
#   long time (confirmed empirically -- see below). repair_timing's finer per-pass bounds
#   (-max_iterations, -max_repairs_per_pass) exist ONLY on OpenROAD 26Q2, NOT on the bundled
#   OpenROAD (edf00dff) -- see the full per-toolchain repair_timing flag comparison in
#   memory/pd/knowledge.md. Rather than hardcode a flag only one toolchain supports, this
#   passthrough lets the caller supply whatever flag(s) their toolchain actually offers.
#
# Both variables are declared IDENTICALLY (byte-for-byte, including description text) on
# OpenROAD.ResizerTimingPostCTS and OpenROAD.ResizerTimingPostGRT in steps/openroad.py, because
# both steps are members of the same Classic flow and Flow.get_all_config_variables() raises
# FlowException if two steps in the same flow declare a same-named Variable with any field
# difference (see librelane/flows/flow.py's get_all_config_variables).
#
# Base: relative to the live tree's state immediately before this bead's apply (i.e. AFTER the
# pre-existing local patches -- PSM parser, ioplacer, pdn.tcl, set_rc.tcl, drt.tcl catch-wrapper,
# Synlig/UHDM fixes -- and BEFORE librelane2413_sta_26q2_scenes.diff, though both patches were
# applied together in the same session and neither depends on the other's file regions).
#
# Validation (bead bpp, 2026-09-15):
#   - Tcl scripts execute without error on the bundled toolchain (edf00dff) -- proven by two
#     real ASAP7 CPU-block runs (rv32i_cpu_top, 36k+ instances, 10 SRAM macros), not a synthetic
#     syntax check:
#       1. `-c PL_RESIZER_TIMING_MAX_PASSES=1` alone: repair_timing's setup call (27550 initial
#          violators) printed WNS/TNS/violator-count rows past "Iter 2000" WITHIN that ONE pass
#          (confirming "Iter" in the printed progress table is a finer-grained counter than
#          "passes" -- NOT bounded by -max_passes), but the pass itself completed and the
#          overall step then exited 0 with `[WARNING RSZ-0062] Unable to repair all setup
#          violations` -- exactly the graceful bounded-stop behaviour wanted, in ~4.5 minutes
#          wall-clock on a design whose repair WAS making genuine progress (27550 -> 924
#          violators before the pass ended).
#       2. `-c PL_RESIZER_TIMING_MAX_PASSES=1 -c 'PL_RESIZER_TIMING_EXTRA_ARGS=-repair_tns 5'`
#          (fresh full run --to OpenROAD.ResizerTimingPostCTS, run bpp5_extraargs_fresh):
#          `Flow complete`, RSZ-0062 bounded stop again (2761 initial violators -> 2219 remaining
#          when the pass ended), hold repair converged separately (WNS +10.675 ps), confirmed via
#          the step's generated _env.tcl: `set ::env(PL_RESIZER_TIMING_MAX_PASSES) 1` and
#          `set ::env(PL_RESIZER_TIMING_EXTRA_ARGS) "-repair_tns 5"` -- and no Tcl argument-parse
#          error (repair_timing's own sta::parse_key_args would raise loudly on an unrecognized
#          key; -repair_tns is a recognized key on BOTH toolchains).
#   - valu_rtl (does not reach either resizer step) re-run with both variables unset: byte-
#     identical metrics to the pre-amendment baseline (54634 inst / 4655.14614 um^2 / WS
#     -54.29279270097747 ps), confirming the two new Variable declarations do not collide at
#     Flow-init time and the guards are true no-ops when unset.
#   - NOT separately proven: that -max_passes / EXTRA_ARGS bounds w3a's ACTUAL stuck design
#     (the SoC with the pinned sram_controller flop) -- this bead's evidence is all on the
#     isolated ASAP7 CPU block, which does not reproduce that specific oscillation. w3a should
#     treat this as a general-purpose bound, not a guaranteed fix for that specific flop.
#
# repair_timing flag comparison (from `info body repair_timing` on each binary; recorded in full
# in memory/pd/knowledge.md):
#   26Q2 keys:  -setup_margin -hold_margin -slack_margin -libraries -max_utilization
#               -max_buffer_percent -sequence -phases -recover_power -repair_tns -max_passes
#               -max_iterations -max_repairs_per_pass
#   26Q2 flags: -setup -hold -allow_setup_violations -skip_pin_swap -skip_gate_cloning
#               -skip_size_down -skip_buffering -skip_buffer_removal -skip_last_gasp
#               -skip_vt_swap -skip_crit_vt_swap -match_cell_footprint -verbose
#   bundled (edf00dff) keys:  -setup_margin -hold_margin -slack_margin -libraries
#               -max_utilization -max_buffer_percent -recover_power -repair_tns -max_passes
#   bundled (edf00dff) flags: -setup -hold -allow_setup_violations -skip_pin_swap
#               -skip_gate_cloning -skip_buffering -skip_buffer_removal -verbose
#   26Q2-only: -sequence -phases -max_iterations -max_repairs_per_pass -skip_size_down
#              -skip_last_gasp -skip_vt_swap -skip_crit_vt_swap -match_cell_footprint

diff -u a/librelane/scripts/openroad/rsz_timing_postcts.tcl b/librelane/scripts/openroad/rsz_timing_postcts.tcl
--- a/librelane/scripts/openroad/rsz_timing_postcts.tcl	2026-03-14 10:53:58.510115756 +0700
+++ b/librelane/scripts/openroad/rsz_timing_postcts.tcl	2026-09-15 23:41:26.051097053 +0700
@@ -37,6 +37,29 @@
 if { $::env(PL_RESIZER_GATE_CLONING) != 1 } {
     lappend setup_args -skip_gate_cloning
 }
+# claude_verilog_test local patch (bead bpp, 2026-09-15): bound repair_timing's pass count so a
+# stuck design (e.g. an unfixable violator that never converges) cannot loop indefinitely and
+# block the flow. Optional -- unset (the default) is BYTE-IDENTICAL to upstream: repair_timing's
+# own default is -max_passes 10000 either way, this only lets a caller override it lower.
+# Confirmed present with identical semantics (bounds rsz::repair_setup/repair_hold's pass count,
+# same default of 10000) on both the bundled OpenROAD (edf00dff) and OpenROAD 26Q2 -- see bead
+# bpp / memory/pd/knowledge.md.
+if { [info exists ::env(PL_RESIZER_TIMING_MAX_PASSES)] && $::env(PL_RESIZER_TIMING_MAX_PASSES) != "" } {
+    lappend setup_args -max_passes $::env(PL_RESIZER_TIMING_MAX_PASSES)
+}
+# claude_verilog_test local patch (bead bpp, 2026-09-15, amendment): -max_passes alone is not
+# sufficient -- a "pass" is a coarse unit, and a design oscillating without progress (e.g. one
+# unfixable violator that repair keeps retrying) can make a SINGLE pass run for a very long time
+# regardless of -max_passes. repair_timing's finer per-pass bounds (-max_iterations,
+# -max_repairs_per_pass) exist ONLY on OpenROAD 26Q2, not on the bundled OpenROAD (edf00dff) --
+# see memory/pd/knowledge.md for the full per-toolchain repair_timing flag comparison. Rather
+# than hardcode a flag only one toolchain supports, this is a GENERIC opt-in passthrough: the
+# env var's value is spliced verbatim (as a Tcl word list) onto the end of the setup repair_timing
+# call, so a caller can pass whatever flag(s) their toolchain actually offers (e.g.
+# "-max_iterations 50" on 26Q2). Optional -- unset (the default) is BYTE-IDENTICAL to upstream.
+if { [info exists ::env(PL_RESIZER_TIMING_EXTRA_ARGS)] && $::env(PL_RESIZER_TIMING_EXTRA_ARGS) != "" } {
+    lappend setup_args {*}$::env(PL_RESIZER_TIMING_EXTRA_ARGS)
+}
 
 set hold_args [list]
 lappend hold_args -verbose
@@ -47,6 +70,12 @@
 if { $::env(PL_RESIZER_ALLOW_SETUP_VIOS) == 1 } {
     lappend hold_args -allow_setup_violations
 }
+# claude_verilog_test local patch (bead bpp, 2026-09-15): see the matching setup_args comment
+# above -- same optional, backward-compatible bound applied to the hold-repair call too, since
+# either direction can be the one that loops.
+if { [info exists ::env(PL_RESIZER_TIMING_MAX_PASSES)] && $::env(PL_RESIZER_TIMING_MAX_PASSES) != "" } {
+    lappend hold_args -max_passes $::env(PL_RESIZER_TIMING_MAX_PASSES)
+}
 
 if { $::env(PL_RESIZER_FIX_HOLD_FIRST) == 1 } {
     repair_timing {*}$hold_args

